1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:06,000
From San Diego to Sweden, they churn out food for the masses.

2
00:00:06,001 --> 00:00:08,000
What type of combo would you like?

3
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:14,000
Where seconds equal dollars, tech innovation rules, and flipping burgers is a thing of the past.

4
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:21,000
These industry giants grow it, process it, freeze it, ship it, track it, fry it, and pack it.

5
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:24,000
All so we can pay them to eat it.

6
00:00:24,001 --> 00:00:25,000
Have a great day.

7
00:00:25,001 --> 00:00:26,000
You too.

8
00:00:26,001 --> 00:00:29,920
Now fast food tech, BillionServed on Modern Marvels.

9
00:00:41,860 --> 00:00:44,600
Lunch rush at a busy McDonald's in Oak Brook, Illinois.

10
00:00:45,680 --> 00:00:50,280
In two hours, about 40 employees will serve as many as 550 meals.

11
00:00:50,281 --> 00:00:54,420
Worldwide, in over 31,000 locations,

12
00:00:54,520 --> 00:00:58,460
McDonald's serves 50 million customers each day.

13
00:00:58,560 --> 00:01:02,360
On an annual basis, we serve over 800 million pounds of beef.

14
00:01:02,460 --> 00:01:06,360
We serve over 600 million eggs, all of that on top of the fact

15
00:01:06,460 --> 00:01:09,600
that we serve over 3 billion pounds of our world-famous

16
00:01:09,700 --> 00:01:11,100
French fries.

17
00:01:11,200 --> 00:01:14,340
With eateries in well over 100 countries,

18
00:01:14,440 --> 00:01:16,840
McDonald's reigns as the undisputed champion

19
00:01:16,940 --> 00:01:20,280
of fast food ubiquity.

20
00:01:20,281 --> 00:01:23,760
But it's not easy to please a world full of culturally varied palates.

21
00:01:25,340 --> 00:01:29,140
McDonald's business is really largely based on a core menu product,

22
00:01:29,280 --> 00:01:32,240
which would be Big Macs and burgers and fries and shakes.

23
00:01:32,720 --> 00:01:35,360
However, occasionally in international markets,

24
00:01:35,460 --> 00:01:39,200
we'll go out and develop products that are really developed specifically for a local taste.

25
00:01:39,420 --> 00:01:43,320
Examples of that may be the Maharaja Mac, which is served in India,

26
00:01:43,321 --> 00:01:45,720
which is actually a chicken-based version of the Big Mac.

27
00:01:46,120 --> 00:01:49,900
Things like the Teriyaki Burger, which is a pork-based product that's served in Japan.

28
00:01:50,280 --> 00:01:52,740
and various rice products that are served in other places

29
00:01:52,741 --> 00:01:55,420
across Asia.

30
00:01:55,421 --> 00:01:57,220
If you live in the Western world,

31
00:01:57,221 --> 00:02:01,860
odds are good you've eaten McDonald's food at least once.

32
00:02:01,861 --> 00:02:03,720
And if you're like most Americans,

33
00:02:03,721 --> 00:02:08,960
you probably eat some kind of fast food at least once a week.

34
00:02:08,961 --> 00:02:12,440
In fact, over 75% of all restaurant visits

35
00:02:12,441 --> 00:02:15,040
happen at a fast food restaurant.

36
00:02:15,041 --> 00:02:16,040
Nobody wants to cook.

37
00:02:16,041 --> 00:02:18,040
We want to find someone else to do the cooking.

38
00:02:18,041 --> 00:02:21,040
And the answer to that question has been a restaurant,

39
00:02:21,540 --> 00:02:24,280
an inexpensive restaurant, a fast food restaurant

40
00:02:24,281 --> 00:02:26,240
who provides us with foods that we actually like.

41
00:02:27,160 --> 00:02:28,280
And since the beginning,

42
00:02:28,780 --> 00:02:30,660
the quintessential fast food combination

43
00:02:30,661 --> 00:02:33,520
has been the burger, fries, and shake.

44
00:02:34,220 --> 00:02:37,320
I believe the burger, fry, and soft drink or shake

45
00:02:37,321 --> 00:02:39,060
is the number one meal ordered in America

46
00:02:39,061 --> 00:02:40,360
because they're a complete meal.

47
00:02:40,500 --> 00:02:42,000
It's not easy to make in your house,

48
00:02:42,100 --> 00:02:44,440
and yet they remain very inexpensive and portable.

49
00:02:45,500 --> 00:02:47,880
And at the center of this complete meal,

50
00:02:48,040 --> 00:02:51,480
All hail the mighty burger.

51
00:02:55,320 --> 00:03:03,440
Today, McDonald's and its competitors depend on evolving technologies to deliver burger after burger after burger.

52
00:03:04,080 --> 00:03:10,320
Folks talk about flipping burgers, and with a technology that we introduced a number of years ago, we actually don't flip them any longer.

53
00:03:10,740 --> 00:03:13,000
This is a grill that we call the clamshell grill.

54
00:03:13,001 --> 00:03:16,060
It actually cooks burgers on both sides at the same time.

55
00:03:16,061 --> 00:03:18,060
It'll take regular hamburger patties

56
00:03:18,061 --> 00:03:20,060
and cook them in less than half the time

57
00:03:20,061 --> 00:03:23,060
that it used to with our previous grills.

58
00:03:23,061 --> 00:03:25,060
It'll be somewhere near 40 seconds

59
00:03:25,061 --> 00:03:26,060
that this product will be actually completed

60
00:03:26,061 --> 00:03:28,060
and ready to be served.

61
00:03:28,061 --> 00:03:30,060
The cook time for these same patties

62
00:03:30,061 --> 00:03:32,060
used to be something a little under two minutes.

63
00:03:34,060 --> 00:03:38,060
And here come four perfectly cooked patties.

64
00:03:38,061 --> 00:03:40,060
This is our rapid toaster.

65
00:03:40,061 --> 00:03:42,060
We drop the bun into the toaster.

66
00:03:42,061 --> 00:03:49,060
And 17 seconds later, we have a perfectly toasted bun.

67
00:03:49,061 --> 00:03:56,060
Since this is all happening simultaneously, you're looking at raw meat to finished burger

68
00:03:56,061 --> 00:03:57,521
in a little over a minute.

69
00:03:57,522 --> 00:04:01,060
Of course, at that speed, you'll need something to wash it down

70
00:04:01,061 --> 00:04:01,761
and fast.

71
00:04:01,762 --> 00:04:08,060
This piece of equipment is probably the crew's favorite technology, the automated beverage dispenser.

72
00:04:08,061 --> 00:04:11,060
This machine is actually tied to our POS equipment.

73
00:04:11,061 --> 00:04:14,060
Now, when a customer in the drive-thru orders the products,

74
00:04:14,061 --> 00:04:17,060
it knows what size they've ordered, whether they want ice,

75
00:04:17,061 --> 00:04:20,060
no ice, lots of ice, a little bit of ice, and actually pours the

76
00:04:20,061 --> 00:04:22,060
product for the crew person.

77
00:04:22,061 --> 00:04:25,060
This piece of equipment will pour more than 5,000 drinks per

78
00:04:25,061 --> 00:04:27,060
week.

79
00:04:27,061 --> 00:04:29,060
But back to burgers.

80
00:04:29,061 --> 00:04:32,060
McDonald's big seller is the Big Mac.

81
00:04:32,061 --> 00:04:36,060
Consumers buy over $550 million each year, just in the United

82
00:04:36,061 --> 00:04:37,060
States.

83
00:04:37,061 --> 00:04:42,060
The Big Mac is a to all beef patties, special sauce, lettuce, cheese, pickles, onion, and

84
00:04:42,061 --> 00:04:43,060
a sesame seed bun.

85
00:04:43,061 --> 00:04:48,060
The sauce is clearly a secret sauce, and it wouldn't be much of a secret if I told you.

86
00:04:48,061 --> 00:04:52,060
But maybe the liquid production people who make the sauce will tell us.

87
00:04:52,061 --> 00:04:57,060
For the Big Mac sauce, I can tell you we use soybean oil and eggs, but I can't tell you

88
00:04:57,061 --> 00:04:58,060
anything after that.

89
00:04:58,061 --> 00:05:05,060
As far as the quantities of Big Mac, we do 15 million tubes annually at this facility,

90
00:05:05,061 --> 00:05:08,700
which equates to 600 million shots,

91
00:05:08,701 --> 00:05:11,800
which serves 300 million sandwiches.

92
00:05:12,940 --> 00:05:16,940
In the kitchen, a worker places a tube in the sauce gun.

93
00:05:16,941 --> 00:05:18,640
Squeeze the trigger and the gun releases

94
00:05:18,641 --> 00:05:20,200
a perfect portion of sauce.

95
00:05:21,180 --> 00:05:23,740
The iconic Big Mac double-decker style sandwich

96
00:05:23,741 --> 00:05:27,840
was invented in 1967 by McDonald's franchisee,

97
00:05:27,841 --> 00:05:30,420
Jim Delligotti of Uniontown, Pennsylvania.

98
00:05:32,420 --> 00:05:34,020
When I started, they said I had to work

99
00:05:34,021 --> 00:05:37,120
with what we have there.

100
00:05:37,121 --> 00:05:39,720
So I wasn't allowed to bring any other bun or anything in.

101
00:05:39,721 --> 00:05:44,100
So I think for one day or two days, I forget which it was,

102
00:05:44,101 --> 00:05:47,160
I did it on a regular hamburger bun.

103
00:05:47,161 --> 00:05:48,400
But it was just too sloppy.

104
00:05:48,401 --> 00:05:50,640
So I went out and got the double cut bun.

105
00:05:50,641 --> 00:05:54,940
And from then on, it's history.

106
00:05:54,941 --> 00:05:56,940
The history of fast food itself

107
00:05:56,941 --> 00:05:59,880
is linked to another industry that highlights speed,

108
00:05:59,881 --> 00:06:01,320
convenience, and affordability.

109
00:06:01,321 --> 00:06:07,400
The burgeoning post-war car culture spawned a new kind of place to eat.

110
00:06:09,780 --> 00:06:14,760
Drive-in restaurants sprouted up all over America, most notably on the West Coast.

111
00:06:16,380 --> 00:06:20,380
They may have been a lot of fun, but drive-ins weren't all that fast.

112
00:06:21,080 --> 00:06:25,240
The fast food joint as we know it can be traced back to a source with a now familiar name.

113
00:06:26,760 --> 00:06:31,100
Back in 1948, the McDonald's brothers actually opened the first restaurant

114
00:06:31,101 --> 00:06:32,700
in San Bernardino, California.

115
00:06:35,360 --> 00:06:37,120
Inspired by Henry Ford,

116
00:06:37,740 --> 00:06:39,680
the McDonald's replaced short-order cooks

117
00:06:39,681 --> 00:06:40,900
with unskilled workers,

118
00:06:41,280 --> 00:06:43,160
each completing one step of the process,

119
00:06:43,580 --> 00:06:44,640
like an assembly line,

120
00:06:45,540 --> 00:06:47,640
making the food fast and consistent.

121
00:06:49,020 --> 00:06:52,280
They cut costs by eliminating carhops and waitresses,

122
00:06:52,940 --> 00:06:54,000
plates and silverware,

123
00:06:54,001 --> 00:06:56,040
and thus dishwashers,

124
00:06:56,300 --> 00:06:57,960
making the food less expensive.

125
00:06:57,961 --> 00:07:04,220
Years later, a milkshake mixer salesman would buy out the McDonald brothers and use their

126
00:07:04,221 --> 00:07:11,860
food service model to spin the business into hamburger gold.

127
00:07:11,861 --> 00:07:16,580
So where does all this fast food and drink come from?

128
00:07:16,581 --> 00:07:21,480
To facilitate such a mammoth operation, McDonald's partners with companies like Golden State

129
00:07:21,481 --> 00:07:26,040
Foods Distribution Centers.

130
00:07:26,041 --> 00:07:31,714
Golden State Foods operates 12 distribution centers that service McDonald's in several U.S.

131
00:07:31,715 --> 00:07:34,520
regions, as well as about 20 other countries.

132
00:07:37,400 --> 00:07:41,920
This is their largest facility, located in City of Industry, California.

133
00:07:42,640 --> 00:07:49,560
This location services over 1,000 McDonald's restaurants in the Southern California market and Las Vegas market.

134
00:07:49,561 --> 00:07:56,940
And because of the proximity of the Los Angeles ports, we also prep shipments to our overseas customers.

135
00:07:57,600 --> 00:08:03,160
This facility ships out on a weekly basis about 45,000 cases of French fries,

136
00:08:03,580 --> 00:08:10,680
which equates to about 1,000,000.6 pounds, about 17 million individual servings of French fries.

137
00:08:10,980 --> 00:08:15,460
This location also ships out 7,000 cases of cheese,

138
00:08:15,461 --> 00:08:19,480
which equates to 8.9 million individual slices.

139
00:08:20,120 --> 00:08:24,680
All of that's in addition to the 900,000 pounds of beef set out each week.

140
00:08:26,280 --> 00:08:28,440
And of course, something for the kids.

141
00:08:28,980 --> 00:08:33,600
What you're seeing here is 75,000 square feet of McDonald's Happy Meal toys.

142
00:08:33,900 --> 00:08:39,000
We distribute 650 million units a year, just like this one.

143
00:08:39,260 --> 00:08:42,620
This makes McDonald's one of the largest toy distributorships in the world.

144
00:08:42,621 --> 00:08:49,700
Golden State Foods also stocks trash bags, cash bags, packaging, cleaning supplies.

145
00:08:50,640 --> 00:08:53,960
If you see it in a McDonald's, it came from a place like this.

146
00:08:55,240 --> 00:09:02,320
This distribution center stocks about 900 different items that are provided by over 40 different suppliers.

147
00:09:03,260 --> 00:09:11,320
You can imagine what a McDonald's lot would look like if all 40 of those suppliers tried to make individual deliveries.

148
00:09:12,620 --> 00:09:16,560
goods arrived by the truckload from the various growers and manufacturers

149
00:09:16,561 --> 00:09:19,700
and are inspected thoroughly for contamination.

150
00:09:22,140 --> 00:09:25,140
Then workers sort them into one of three main areas,

151
00:09:25,780 --> 00:09:28,580
dry products, refrigerated, and frozen.

152
00:09:29,520 --> 00:09:32,020
We're in the refrigerator compartment of the facility.

153
00:09:32,520 --> 00:09:34,460
This is a 38-degree temperature.

154
00:09:35,020 --> 00:09:37,360
This is where we keep eggs for breakfast,

155
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:41,260
cheese for our sandwiches, and produce for our salads.

156
00:09:41,261 --> 00:09:43,260
This is the freezer.

157
00:09:43,261 --> 00:09:46,260
This is maintained at below zero.

158
00:09:46,261 --> 00:09:50,260
This is where we keep the protein items, the beef patties,

159
00:09:50,261 --> 00:09:52,260
the chicken products, and of course,

160
00:09:52,261 --> 00:09:55,260
the famous French fries from McDonald's.

161
00:09:55,261 --> 00:10:00,260
Restaurants send in their orders, and technology takes over.

162
00:10:00,261 --> 00:10:04,260
The order is downloaded via radio frequency from the mainframe

163
00:10:04,261 --> 00:10:08,260
to the actual order selectors' headsets,

164
00:10:08,261 --> 00:10:12,440
and the order is directed to the order selector via voice.

165
00:10:12,441 --> 00:10:13,600
3059.

166
00:10:13,601 --> 00:10:18,200
And they go directly to the order selection location,

167
00:10:18,201 --> 00:10:22,700
and they're specifying the quantity of the item to select

168
00:10:22,701 --> 00:10:24,740
and the type of product to select,

169
00:10:24,741 --> 00:10:27,140
and verification is made via voice.

170
00:10:27,141 --> 00:10:31,040
Workers load the specified products onto pallets,

171
00:10:31,041 --> 00:10:33,780
which are then forklifted onto delivery trucks.

172
00:10:33,781 --> 00:10:39,260
from production to distribution to consumption.

173
00:10:40,120 --> 00:10:43,220
McDonald's operates like a well-oiled machine.

174
00:10:44,360 --> 00:10:46,900
And though it may be the proverbial 800-pound gorilla,

175
00:10:47,660 --> 00:10:49,940
it isn't the only burger slinger in town.

176
00:10:51,460 --> 00:10:55,380
Competitors like Wendy's, Jack in the Box, Burger King, and Carl's Jr.

177
00:10:55,920 --> 00:10:59,940
also take a big bite out of the fast food biz using various strategies.

178
00:11:00,820 --> 00:11:02,640
Find a niche and fill it.

179
00:11:02,641 --> 00:11:05,880
In one instance, fill it really, really full.

180
00:11:07,080 --> 00:11:10,240
In a world of obesity awareness and burger bashing,

181
00:11:10,940 --> 00:11:12,500
Carl's Jr. is on the offensive,

182
00:11:13,500 --> 00:11:15,340
building burgers bigger and badder,

183
00:11:15,800 --> 00:11:18,640
topping them with gooey sauces, fried onion rings,

184
00:11:19,080 --> 00:11:23,180
slabs of pastrami, layers of bacon, and gobs of guacamole.

185
00:11:23,640 --> 00:11:26,800
So this is the Santa Fe sauce.

186
00:11:26,880 --> 00:11:28,720
It goes on both sides of the bun.

187
00:11:28,721 --> 00:11:42,721
Then we add lettuce, two tomato slices, red onion, a half pound of Angus beef, two slices of pepper jack cheese, two slices of bacon, and two scoops of guacamole.

188
00:11:46,640 --> 00:11:49,880
Not enough meat for you? How about this carnivore's delight?

189
00:11:50,580 --> 00:11:55,100
Two quarter pound patties and pastrami.

190
00:11:55,101 --> 00:12:02,920
Jay Leno joked about it on The Tonight Show, saying that what's the world coming to when

191
00:12:02,921 --> 00:12:06,160
meat is now a condiment for more meat?

192
00:12:06,161 --> 00:12:08,340
So where will this mega burger mania end?

193
00:12:08,341 --> 00:12:13,100
Well, if I were ready to make an outrageous burger, I would put all the proteins together.

194
00:12:13,101 --> 00:12:21,080
Hamburger, chicken, fish, cheese, maybe breakfast sausage, bacon.

195
00:12:21,081 --> 00:12:23,780
It would be awesomely bad.

196
00:12:25,100 --> 00:12:29,860
But a gigantic burger does not a perfect fast food meal make.

197
00:12:30,800 --> 00:12:32,340
Would you like fries with that?

198
00:12:34,860 --> 00:12:40,480
According to McDonald's, the weight of sesame seeds used in a year on Big Macs sold in the U.S.

199
00:12:40,900 --> 00:12:45,260
equals the weight of more than two Boeing 747 airplanes.

200
00:12:46,820 --> 00:12:51,800
Fast Food Tech will return on Modern Marvels here on the History Channel.

201
00:12:55,100 --> 00:13:02,060
First introduced to Americans by Thomas Jefferson, the simple fried potato stick has become a fast food staple.

202
00:13:03,100 --> 00:13:07,180
Known today as the French fry, it's the quintessential side dish.

203
00:13:07,900 --> 00:13:12,540
Delicious, mass produced, and massively profitable for fast food restaurants.

204
00:13:14,040 --> 00:13:15,784
More than a million acres of U.S.

205
00:13:15,785 --> 00:13:19,800
land, about the size of Rhode Island, are devoted to the growth of potatoes.

206
00:13:19,801 --> 00:13:25,700
And 35% of the harvest goes to making frozen french fries.

207
00:13:26,080 --> 00:13:29,180
These potatoes in this particular field today are russet burbac.

208
00:13:29,720 --> 00:13:32,440
This particular potato here is about a 10-ounce potato.

209
00:13:32,700 --> 00:13:35,520
We could get approximately 100 strips out of this potato.

210
00:13:36,780 --> 00:13:41,680
A little bigger potato, we'd get maybe 125, 130 strips out of one potato.

211
00:13:41,880 --> 00:13:45,980
But this is just about the perfect-sized potato for making french fries right here.

212
00:13:45,981 --> 00:13:52,980
The Ochoa Foods potato processing plant in Warden, Washington, converts 2 million pounds

213
00:13:52,981 --> 00:14:00,240
of potatoes into nearly 100 million French fries each day.

214
00:14:00,241 --> 00:14:05,100
The potatoes are brought in from the field and separated by size.

215
00:14:05,101 --> 00:14:07,600
After an initial wash, they're off to the steam peeler.

216
00:14:07,601 --> 00:14:10,400
It's about an 1800 liter vessel.

217
00:14:10,401 --> 00:14:14,540
We put in about 800 pounds of potatoes and turn the steam on.

218
00:14:14,541 --> 00:14:19,440
It's about 250 psi of steam, which equates to about 405 degrees.

219
00:14:20,440 --> 00:14:24,160
As the drum rotates, steam enters through a high-pressure valve.

220
00:14:25,340 --> 00:14:31,960
When that pressure is rapidly released, it causes an explosion that blows the peels away from the surface of the potatoes.

221
00:14:33,660 --> 00:14:35,760
They're in there for about 10 seconds.

222
00:14:37,560 --> 00:14:42,440
After a quick rinse in the barrel washer to get rid of the loose skins, it's time for inspection.

223
00:14:42,441 --> 00:14:49,860
workers scrutinize the load and trim away any foreign material defects and generally ugly bits

224
00:14:49,861 --> 00:14:56,860
the potatoes are then once again separated by size in preparation for the water cutter

225
00:14:56,861 --> 00:15:05,220
the water cutter is basically a potato cannon water pumps through it at about 50 miles per hour

226
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:11,640
the pressure forces the potatoes one at a time through the pipe toward a stationary grid of

227
00:15:11,641 --> 00:15:18,880
blades. One whole potato goes in, 100 potato strips come out.

228
00:15:18,881 --> 00:15:21,740
The starchy potato and water soup shoots out onto a

229
00:15:21,741 --> 00:15:27,700
conveyor in the dewatering area. Next up, more quality control.

230
00:15:27,701 --> 00:15:31,500
This is our grating deck. The first set of rollers back here

231
00:15:31,501 --> 00:15:34,600
is where we take the slivers out. Then we go on to the

232
00:15:34,601 --> 00:15:38,200
grater shaker that takes the nubbins and the short pieces

233
00:15:38,201 --> 00:15:43,200
The next conveyor carries the fries past a high-res camera

234
00:15:43,201 --> 00:15:46,200
that identifies visible defects.

235
00:15:46,201 --> 00:15:50,200
Then the automatic defect removal system uses bursts of air

236
00:15:50,201 --> 00:15:52,200
to knock out the bad ones.

237
00:15:52,201 --> 00:15:55,200
A cutter slices out the imperfection.

238
00:15:55,201 --> 00:15:58,200
The fries that make the grade travel to the blanching area

239
00:15:58,201 --> 00:16:00,200
for a hot bath.

240
00:16:00,201 --> 00:16:04,200
Here the fries submerge into 160-inch

241
00:16:04,201 --> 00:16:13,640
Here the fries submerge into 170 to 180 degree water for 11 to 16 minutes.

242
00:16:13,641 --> 00:16:19,020
This will allow the starch granules within the potato cells to swell up, giving the strips

243
00:16:19,021 --> 00:16:23,860
a mealy, fluffy texture.

244
00:16:23,861 --> 00:16:28,520
No one wants soggy fries, so a trip through the dehydrating tunnel removes the excess

245
00:16:28,521 --> 00:16:29,520
moisture.

246
00:16:29,521 --> 00:16:30,776
This is our dryer.

247
00:16:30,777 --> 00:16:35,660
We evaporate about 9,000 pounds of water per hour through this system.

248
00:16:36,960 --> 00:16:41,100
By this point in the process, after peeling, blanching, and dehydrating,

249
00:16:41,860 --> 00:16:45,680
100 pounds of potatoes will yield about 55 pounds of fries.

250
00:16:47,740 --> 00:16:51,160
Now, a final round of inspection and into the fryer.

251
00:16:53,600 --> 00:16:57,640
Partially frying the potatoes in 375-degree soybean oil

252
00:16:57,641 --> 00:17:01,780
dramatically cuts down the cooking time on the restaurant end.

253
00:17:01,880 --> 00:17:04,780
After all, it is fast food.

254
00:17:04,880 --> 00:17:06,880
A conveyor then carries the hot potatoes

255
00:17:06,980 --> 00:17:12,980
through a 180-foot tunnel, kept at minus 35 degrees.

256
00:17:13,080 --> 00:17:16,400
Flash freezing helps sustain shelf life.

257
00:17:16,500 --> 00:17:19,300
The fries emerge at 10 degrees Fahrenheit,

258
00:17:19,400 --> 00:17:23,300
ready for packaging and cold storage.

259
00:17:23,400 --> 00:17:24,840
This is the freezer here.

260
00:17:24,940 --> 00:17:26,900
It's five degrees below zero.

261
00:17:26,901 --> 00:17:31,060
We have about 30 to 33 million pounds of french fries in here.

262
00:17:31,480 --> 00:17:35,900
Takes us about 30 to 35 days to turn this freezer over.

263
00:17:37,220 --> 00:17:42,120
A potato's journey from delivery to storage takes one hour and 10 minutes.

264
00:17:42,920 --> 00:17:47,200
About 25% of Ochoa's frozen fries continue their journey to Wendy's.

265
00:17:49,460 --> 00:17:54,900
Most chains serve this kind of frozen potato fried in some sort of vegetable oil in similar fryers.

266
00:17:56,900 --> 00:18:01,900
Still, each claims to have the secret for perfect fries.

267
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:05,200
This particular development is a frozen fry dispenser

268
00:18:05,300 --> 00:18:09,040
that will hold up to 60 pounds of frozen French fries

269
00:18:09,140 --> 00:18:10,640
yet ready to be cooked.

270
00:18:10,740 --> 00:18:16,660
We simply take a basket, place it in the dispenser.

271
00:18:16,760 --> 00:18:19,860
An electric eye will know that the fries, the fry basket

272
00:18:19,960 --> 00:18:21,160
has been placed there.

273
00:18:21,260 --> 00:18:26,400
We'll then put the fries into our high efficiency gas fryer.

274
00:18:26,401 --> 00:18:29,940
And the computer actually begins to count down the amount of time

275
00:18:29,941 --> 00:18:32,080
it takes for these fries to be cooked.

276
00:18:32,081 --> 00:18:36,340
Dump, salt, scoop, and serve.

277
00:18:36,341 --> 00:18:37,980
The specially designed scooping tool

278
00:18:37,981 --> 00:18:41,140
makes the perfectly portioned treats stand at attention,

279
00:18:41,141 --> 00:18:43,120
ready to take their place next to the burger.

280
00:18:47,180 --> 00:18:49,000
America's love affair with French fries

281
00:18:49,001 --> 00:18:51,360
began when Jefferson served them at Monticello

282
00:18:51,361 --> 00:18:55,160
after his ambassadorship to France, hence their name.

283
00:18:56,400 --> 00:19:01,400
Today, McDonald's is the largest single buyer of potatoes in the United States,

284
00:19:01,401 --> 00:19:06,400
which means it'll need a whole lot of ketchup for dipping.

285
00:19:06,401 --> 00:19:09,400
I'd like some fries, please, with extra ketchup.

286
00:19:09,401 --> 00:19:12,400
So it's back to the liquid production facility,

287
00:19:12,401 --> 00:19:17,400
where rows of nozzles deposit ketchup into packets of all sizes.

288
00:19:17,401 --> 00:19:23,400
In this facility, we package ketchup from 9 grams all the way up to about 3 gallons.

289
00:19:23,401 --> 00:19:27,400
We package about 15 million ketchup packets a day.

290
00:19:27,401 --> 00:19:31,400
That equals about 4.5 billion packets of ketchup a year.

291
00:19:31,401 --> 00:19:36,400
That's half of the ketchup consumed in McDonald's in the United States every year.

292
00:19:36,401 --> 00:19:40,400
We package a unique formula of ketchup just for McDonald's.

293
00:19:40,401 --> 00:19:45,400
So what is it about french fries, the very smell of which makes us salivate?

294
00:19:45,401 --> 00:19:51,400
It's likely that the smell of cooking starch and fat incites a physical reaction,

295
00:19:51,401 --> 00:19:54,040
because our bodies know that much needed calories

296
00:19:54,140 --> 00:19:58,040
and energy are on the way, an instinct originating

297
00:19:58,140 --> 00:20:01,940
back in history when food was scarce.

298
00:20:02,040 --> 00:20:03,440
We like fried food.

299
00:20:03,540 --> 00:20:05,840
We don't like to say we like fried food, but we do.

300
00:20:05,940 --> 00:20:07,980
If you want a salad, you have it available to you.

301
00:20:08,080 --> 00:20:09,220
You may not choose to eat it.

302
00:20:09,320 --> 00:20:10,880
The moment you walk in there, you may find that, boy,

303
00:20:10,980 --> 00:20:14,360
that burger smells good, and that happens quite a bit.

304
00:20:14,460 --> 00:20:16,600
Now that you have your burger and fries,

305
00:20:16,700 --> 00:20:20,000
let's complete that meal with a concoction that's part milk,

306
00:20:20,001 --> 00:20:25,200
milk, part soft drink, and part dessert, the shake.

307
00:20:25,201 --> 00:20:28,380
Again, each chain wants to stand out.

308
00:20:28,381 --> 00:20:30,100
Our hand-scooped ice cream shakes

309
00:20:30,101 --> 00:20:31,720
are made the old-fashioned way.

310
00:20:31,721 --> 00:20:37,080
We hand-scoop the ice cream, add syrup, milk,

311
00:20:37,081 --> 00:20:40,020
blend it on an old-fashioned ice cream milkshake ledger,

312
00:20:40,021 --> 00:20:43,200
and top it off with whipped cream.

313
00:20:43,201 --> 00:20:45,020
Another company's shake offering

314
00:20:45,021 --> 00:20:49,640
is really more half-shake, half soft-serve ice cream.

315
00:20:49,641 --> 00:20:52,840
Wendy's has had Frosties since the beginning of our existence.

316
00:20:53,580 --> 00:20:55,980
From the longest time, we just had a chocolate Frostie.

317
00:20:56,700 --> 00:20:59,280
In 2007, we added a new flavor, vanilla.

318
00:21:00,060 --> 00:21:03,040
The way it works is we put a mix inside the machine.

319
00:21:03,160 --> 00:21:03,900
It comes in a liquid.

320
00:21:04,080 --> 00:21:05,200
The hopper is refrigerated.

321
00:21:05,800 --> 00:21:08,760
And then it slowly filters into a chamber.

322
00:21:08,761 --> 00:21:11,460
And then once it's frozen and the light's ready to go,

323
00:21:11,980 --> 00:21:15,820
we're able to portion it out into a Frostie.

324
00:21:15,821 --> 00:21:22,820
Company-wide, Wendy's sells about 250 million Frosties annually.

325
00:21:22,821 --> 00:21:25,820
Wendy's has another Frosty product now.

326
00:21:25,821 --> 00:21:34,820
And then we use a spoon, actually, as the mixing mechanism.

327
00:21:34,821 --> 00:21:41,820
We release the spoon.

328
00:21:41,821 --> 00:21:44,960
And you have a Twisted Frosty.

329
00:21:46,660 --> 00:21:48,940
And, of course, there's the McDonald's shake.

330
00:21:49,740 --> 00:21:52,920
The McDonald's shakes are actually made of 100% dairy mix.

331
00:21:53,140 --> 00:21:56,320
A dairy mix is a product that's made of milk and a milk salad.

332
00:21:58,100 --> 00:22:02,080
This prefab dairy mix is combined with liquid milk and flavored syrup,

333
00:22:02,840 --> 00:22:04,980
chilled and dispensed by machine.

334
00:22:06,660 --> 00:22:10,260
This method provides a consistent product and speedy serve time

335
00:22:10,261 --> 00:22:12,340
that the old-fashioned way couldn't match.

336
00:22:14,460 --> 00:22:16,700
It was the old-fashioned milkshake

337
00:22:16,701 --> 00:22:19,500
that first introduced multi-mixer salesman Ray Kroc

338
00:22:19,501 --> 00:22:21,180
to McDonald's in 1954.

339
00:22:22,940 --> 00:22:26,560
While most burger joints in the 1950s had one, maybe two,

340
00:22:27,440 --> 00:22:29,460
McDonald's used eight multi-mixers.

341
00:22:31,760 --> 00:22:34,500
Ray Kroc hopped a plane to California to find out why.

342
00:22:36,120 --> 00:22:38,060
Upon seeing the breakout success

343
00:22:38,061 --> 00:22:39,620
of the innovative restaurant model,

344
00:22:40,260 --> 00:22:43,720
Croc struck a deal to become McDonald's exclusive franchise broker.

345
00:22:45,900 --> 00:22:48,680
With that move, he made business history

346
00:22:48,681 --> 00:22:52,880
by founding the largest, most profitable fast food empire on Earth,

347
00:22:53,740 --> 00:22:56,020
thanks in part to the humble shake.

348
00:22:59,080 --> 00:23:03,600
Burgers, fries, and shakes make up 40% of fast food meals served.

349
00:23:05,140 --> 00:23:06,900
But move over, burger joints.

350
00:23:07,240 --> 00:23:09,020
There's another hombre in town.

351
00:23:09,620 --> 00:23:19,500
In one year, Ochoa Foods produces enough fries to reach from the Earth to the moon and back more than three times.

352
00:23:20,880 --> 00:23:24,060
Fast food tech will return on Modern Marvels.

353
00:23:27,660 --> 00:23:36,440
Burger chains dominate the American landscape, but a growing segment of the quick service restaurant biz belongs to cuisine originating south of the border.

354
00:23:36,441 --> 00:23:41,840
In recent years, Mexican food has taken off because it has a health halo.

355
00:23:42,080 --> 00:23:46,600
It's the idea that those fresh vegetables that were using tortillas, you know,

356
00:23:46,940 --> 00:23:50,400
it's less fried food than something like French fries and a burger.

357
00:23:51,880 --> 00:23:58,200
Taco Bell serves over 2 billion customers each year, resulting in about $6 billion in sales.

358
00:23:59,700 --> 00:24:05,080
Most of that revenue comes from the 2 billion tacos and 1 billion burritos it sells annually.

359
00:24:05,081 --> 00:24:07,140
We sell a lot of food.

360
00:24:07,220 --> 00:24:09,260
When you're going to make that much, it's going to take a lot.

361
00:24:09,440 --> 00:24:14,420
We use over 3.8 billion corn and flour tortillas.

362
00:24:14,640 --> 00:24:17,880
We use 120 million pounds of fresh lettuce,

363
00:24:18,360 --> 00:24:20,800
62 million pounds of pinto beans,

364
00:24:21,360 --> 00:24:24,260
295 million pounds of ground beef,

365
00:24:24,400 --> 00:24:26,540
and 106 million pounds of cheese.

366
00:24:27,920 --> 00:24:30,500
But like many of today's well-known fast food chains,

367
00:24:31,140 --> 00:24:34,280
Taco Bell was small before it became grande.

368
00:24:35,080 --> 00:24:44,000
Mexican restaurant owner Glen Bell started Taco Bell in Downey, California in 1962.

369
00:24:44,001 --> 00:24:48,840
Bell applied McDonald's assembly line process to Mexican food.

370
00:24:48,841 --> 00:24:56,640
Still, tacos aren't burgers, so Bell had to tailor prep tools specific to his menu.

371
00:24:56,641 --> 00:25:01,580
Glen Bell wanted to find a way to make hard shell tacos to fry them quickly, and so he

372
00:25:01,581 --> 00:25:09,660
He went to a chicken coop manufacturer and talked this guy into making the original fry form out of chicken wire.

373
00:25:11,200 --> 00:25:14,900
With this tool, he created a U-shaped hard taco shell.

374
00:25:15,460 --> 00:25:18,520
It's easy to fry, stack, and hold.

375
00:25:20,180 --> 00:25:26,060
Bell soon became a taco titan, whose company is still known for engineering effective and efficient kitchen equipment.

376
00:25:26,061 --> 00:25:34,740
We grill all of our tortillas in our restaurant, and by instituting this grill in our restaurants

377
00:25:34,741 --> 00:25:39,660
versus steaming tortillas in the old days, this process has saved a significant amount

378
00:25:39,661 --> 00:25:40,760
of energy in our restaurants.

379
00:25:41,080 --> 00:25:45,820
We're saving over 100 kilowatt hours every day, which is about a tenth of all of our

380
00:25:45,821 --> 00:25:46,521
energy.

381
00:25:46,940 --> 00:25:50,200
In here is where we hold our hot proteins and sauces.

382
00:25:50,201 --> 00:25:57,620
This saves over 125 gallons of hot water every day versus what Glenn Bell used to use back in the old days.

383
00:25:58,160 --> 00:26:00,080
There was steam bellowing out.

384
00:26:00,520 --> 00:26:02,240
Now today everything is dry.

385
00:26:02,420 --> 00:26:04,660
It's held hotter and more consistently.

386
00:26:05,420 --> 00:26:11,400
And plus we save over 300 million gallons a year of hot water just with this new holding station.

387
00:26:13,120 --> 00:26:16,600
These days many of Taco Bell's products don't need an assembly line.

388
00:26:16,601 --> 00:26:23,920
Thanks to various kitchen innovations implemented over time, the process has been streamlined

389
00:26:23,921 --> 00:26:30,460
to the point that one person can fill an entire order quickly and consistently.

390
00:26:30,461 --> 00:26:36,700
We have portion control devices, for example, like this, versus in Glenville days they had

391
00:26:36,701 --> 00:26:41,680
a pie knife and they would merely grab in the beef and put it on the taco.

392
00:26:41,681 --> 00:26:46,360
We had portion control nacho cheese and red sauce so that we can make sure that we consistently

393
00:26:46,361 --> 00:26:48,440
give the right amount of product.

394
00:26:48,740 --> 00:26:50,660
We have sour cream and guacamole

395
00:26:50,661 --> 00:26:51,880
that is portion controlled.

396
00:26:52,700 --> 00:26:55,120
And all that means you can crank out a fresh taco

397
00:26:55,121 --> 00:26:56,720
really fast.

398
00:26:57,420 --> 00:26:58,900
Crunchy taco begins with

399
00:26:58,901 --> 00:27:00,540
our nice shell here.

400
00:27:00,660 --> 00:27:02,720
We add our beef. Put on an ounce

401
00:27:02,721 --> 00:27:03,800
and a half of beef.

402
00:27:05,020 --> 00:27:06,720
Next, we have a half

403
00:27:06,721 --> 00:27:07,560
an ounce of lettuce.

404
00:27:08,620 --> 00:27:10,840
And then we top it off with cheese.

405
00:27:12,200 --> 00:27:12,900
And that is

406
00:27:12,901 --> 00:27:14,020
our freshly prepared taco.

407
00:27:14,021 --> 00:27:17,760
This taco today is made in 10.7 seconds.

408
00:27:18,620 --> 00:27:20,820
Taco Bell, like other fast food chains,

409
00:27:20,960 --> 00:27:23,620
constantly tries to develop food you can eat on the run.

410
00:27:24,600 --> 00:27:27,360
A perfect example, the Crunchwrap Supreme.

411
00:27:28,060 --> 00:27:30,540
Take one of our 12-inch flour tortillas.

412
00:27:31,140 --> 00:27:33,940
We then top it with a portion of our seasoned beef,

413
00:27:38,080 --> 00:27:39,340
nacho cheese sauce,

414
00:27:39,920 --> 00:27:41,140
with one of our tostada shells,

415
00:27:41,141 --> 00:27:44,260
lettuce, sour cream, and tomatoes.

416
00:27:44,261 --> 00:27:48,940
We then fold the product with our special six-sided

417
00:27:48,941 --> 00:27:56,940
fold that allows us to ensure the product is completely sealed for maximum portability.

418
00:27:56,941 --> 00:28:03,941
We then place the product on the grill for 27 seconds to seal it tight.

419
00:28:03,942 --> 00:28:05,420
Take our crunch

420
00:28:05,421 --> 00:28:14,920
off the grill, slide it inside our easy to eat from bag,

421
00:28:14,921 --> 00:28:18,260
and fold it down to serve.

422
00:28:18,261 --> 00:28:20,660
When you're in the car and you want to eat the product,

423
00:28:20,661 --> 00:28:23,080
all you need to do is open up the bag,

424
00:28:23,081 --> 00:28:27,340
slide the product up, as you can see, nicely sealed,

425
00:28:27,341 --> 00:28:28,740
and you can take a bite and enjoy.

426
00:28:32,520 --> 00:28:35,120
The foil lined bag isn't just portable.

427
00:28:35,121 --> 00:28:37,280
It also helps the product retain heat.

428
00:28:39,400 --> 00:28:42,980
Because of the often unusual shapes and contents of their products,

429
00:28:43,560 --> 00:28:46,560
Taco Bell has to develop packaging specific to their needs.

430
00:28:47,840 --> 00:28:50,100
The wraps are different materials.

431
00:28:50,320 --> 00:28:52,340
Some are thinner than others.

432
00:28:52,540 --> 00:28:57,420
Some are thicker than others based on the protein or the carrier that we're holding it in.

433
00:28:57,540 --> 00:28:59,400
This is used for a soft taco.

434
00:28:59,800 --> 00:29:00,740
It's a pocket.

435
00:29:00,741 --> 00:29:05,740
One use of packaging that's a little unique is our quesadilla.

436
00:29:05,741 --> 00:29:09,740
So here's our fresh grilled quesadilla.

437
00:29:09,741 --> 00:29:11,740
Place it in the wrap.

438
00:29:14,740 --> 00:29:17,740
And then we cut it actually while it's in the wrap.

439
00:29:17,741 --> 00:29:19,740
So we have guides here.

440
00:29:19,741 --> 00:29:21,740
Cut it this way.

441
00:29:21,741 --> 00:29:23,740
Right through the wrap.

442
00:29:26,740 --> 00:29:27,740
Fold it over.

443
00:29:27,741 --> 00:29:29,740
And it's good to go.

444
00:29:29,741 --> 00:29:32,740
But before fast food companies start blowing their profits

445
00:29:32,741 --> 00:29:35,740
designing fancy equipment and packaging,

446
00:29:35,741 --> 00:29:38,740
they first have to invest in making sure you try the food

447
00:29:38,741 --> 00:29:40,740
and that it doesn't make you sick.

448
00:29:44,740 --> 00:29:48,740
If you stack all the flour tortillas Taco Bell serves in a year,

449
00:29:48,741 --> 00:29:52,740
the column would be 3,500 miles high.

450
00:29:52,741 --> 00:29:56,740
Fast Food Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

451
00:29:56,741 --> 00:30:03,700
It doesn't matter if they're selling burgers or tacos, french fries or nachos.

452
00:30:04,000 --> 00:30:07,720
There's some fierce competition for fast food dollars and brand loyalty.

453
00:30:09,480 --> 00:30:14,260
Industry-wide, that translates to advertising costs in the billions of dollars.

454
00:30:16,020 --> 00:30:19,660
Fast food companies are king when it comes to famous marketing strategies.

455
00:30:20,960 --> 00:30:26,440
Studies have shown that Ronald McDonald is the second most recognizable character in the world.

456
00:30:26,740 --> 00:30:28,900
after Santa Claus.

457
00:30:28,901 --> 00:30:31,840
That's proliferation.

458
00:30:31,841 --> 00:30:35,920
While McDonald's aims much of its advertising at families,

459
00:30:35,921 --> 00:30:38,560
Carl's Jr. is known for its racy ads,

460
00:30:38,561 --> 00:30:42,520
targeting another key fast food chomping demographic.

461
00:30:42,521 --> 00:30:45,460
Our target audience we call young hungry guys, which

462
00:30:45,461 --> 00:30:47,860
are guys between the ages of 18 and 34.

463
00:30:47,861 --> 00:30:50,320
They go to fast food more than any other group.

464
00:30:50,321 --> 00:30:53,660
And they really use fast food as an alternative to their kitchen.

465
00:30:53,661 --> 00:30:54,900
They may go in their kitchen, but they

466
00:30:54,901 --> 00:30:56,440
don't know how a lot of the things work in there.

467
00:30:56,441 --> 00:30:57,960
so they use fast food at their kitchen.

468
00:30:59,860 --> 00:31:03,520
Carl's Jr. markets to this group using provocative slogans like,

469
00:31:04,020 --> 00:31:07,020
if it doesn't get all over the place, it doesn't belong in your face.

470
00:31:08,840 --> 00:31:12,540
Making what is jokingly referred to as burger porn.

471
00:31:14,880 --> 00:31:17,680
But before they let you stuff your face with that burger,

472
00:31:18,380 --> 00:31:22,120
it goes through test after test after test.

473
00:31:22,840 --> 00:31:25,840
The first line of defense in any food-related business,

474
00:31:25,841 --> 00:31:28,840
fast or otherwise, is food safety.

475
00:31:30,340 --> 00:31:34,000
Toiling in labs and test kitchens within the walls of corporate HQ,

476
00:31:34,880 --> 00:31:36,960
these workers are on the front lines,

477
00:31:37,700 --> 00:31:39,880
beefing up security in the war on pathogens

478
00:31:39,881 --> 00:31:41,820
found in our favorite quick cuisines.

479
00:31:42,580 --> 00:31:47,540
We turned food safety into a management system called HACCP.

480
00:31:47,800 --> 00:31:49,860
It's an acronym, H-A-C-C-P,

481
00:31:50,220 --> 00:31:52,880
for hazard analysis and critical control points.

482
00:31:52,881 --> 00:31:55,460
A term is used, farm to fork.

483
00:31:55,461 --> 00:32:01,720
You need to start controlling food safety and quality attributes as far up in the supply chain as you can,

484
00:32:01,920 --> 00:32:07,740
and then design and integrate your safety systems all the way through until you deliver the food to a guest.

485
00:32:09,060 --> 00:32:16,600
Before it becomes part of your burger or taco, meat is subjected to some serious science, probing what might be inside.

486
00:32:16,601 --> 00:32:23,615
Any raw meat can contain some level of pathogens, be it salmonella, E.

487
00:32:23,616 --> 00:32:25,820
coli 0157H7, listeria,

488
00:32:26,080 --> 00:32:32,812
camphlobacter, this whole big list of complex names, are part of proteins.

489
00:32:32,813 --> 00:32:34,540
So proper handling,

490
00:32:34,800 --> 00:32:40,440
refrigeration, temperature controls, and then properly cooking are parts of the quality system.

491
00:32:40,441 --> 00:32:45,260
Today, we use gene probe technology like you would see on CSI Miami.

492
00:32:45,560 --> 00:32:51,320
You basically take samples, extract their DNA, break up the DNA chain,

493
00:32:51,440 --> 00:32:53,460
and then you compare those against known standards.

494
00:32:55,020 --> 00:32:59,160
Since Jack in the Box implemented its food safety program in 1993,

495
00:33:00,020 --> 00:33:02,580
many other QSRs have adopted the system,

496
00:33:03,220 --> 00:33:07,300
as have some food retailers, schools, and even the military.

497
00:33:07,301 --> 00:33:13,600
The system has been extremely effective in controlling pathogens and significantly decreasing

498
00:33:13,601 --> 00:33:18,560
risks of contamination.

499
00:33:18,561 --> 00:33:22,240
Another job of corporate fast food test kitchens is to develop new products.

500
00:33:22,241 --> 00:33:27,020
When developing a new product, there are a lot of factors that come into play.

501
00:33:27,021 --> 00:33:32,060
For example, does the technology exist to make the product that we're interested in

502
00:33:32,061 --> 00:33:37,520
making from the supplier side and from our side in terms of operations.

503
00:33:37,521 --> 00:33:40,040
Can we execute this consistently?

504
00:33:40,041 --> 00:33:43,380
Can we make it the quality level that we want?

505
00:33:43,381 --> 00:33:49,380
Can it be sold to the consumer at the right price point?

506
00:33:49,381 --> 00:33:56,060
Once they have the answers, they test the product on the public.

507
00:33:56,061 --> 00:34:01,900
But sometimes after hundreds of tests and months, even years in development, some products

508
00:34:01,901 --> 00:34:02,900
still flop.

509
00:34:02,901 --> 00:34:07,080
One of the greatest stories in fast food is that consumers told McDonald's they wanted

510
00:34:07,081 --> 00:34:10,840
the McLean Deluxe, and it was one of the biggest failures in food service history.

511
00:34:10,841 --> 00:34:15,220
The McLean Deluxe failed because a fat-free burger doesn't taste very good.

512
00:34:15,221 --> 00:34:19,940
What consumers say they want in the test group or in the test kitchen is not necessarily

513
00:34:19,941 --> 00:34:23,460
what they'd want to buy.

514
00:34:23,461 --> 00:34:30,780
Some products end up as limited-time offers or as seasonal menu items that come and go.

515
00:34:31,900 --> 00:34:37,280
One of the more recent sensations to emerge from a fast food kitchen isn't food at all.

516
00:34:38,180 --> 00:34:39,940
Biodiesel is a very interesting subject.

517
00:34:40,620 --> 00:34:45,700
McDonald's is encouraging all of its distributors to be environmentally conscious.

518
00:34:46,380 --> 00:34:54,000
The waste oil that comes out of the fryers in the McDonald's restaurant can be processed and turned into biodiesel fuel.

519
00:34:54,600 --> 00:35:00,080
In the case of this distribution center, for instance, 100% of our fleet burns biodiesel fuel.

520
00:35:00,081 --> 00:35:03,680
It's a blended mix of diesel fuel and biodiesel fuel.

521
00:35:03,681 --> 00:35:07,760
Restaurants are using the oil that's delivered to cook their

522
00:35:07,761 --> 00:35:08,720
french fries.

523
00:35:08,721 --> 00:35:12,400
That oil is then collected, processed, and burned in our

524
00:35:12,401 --> 00:35:14,720
trucks to create cleaner air.

525
00:35:14,721 --> 00:35:20,160
The use of McFuel is still in its infancy, but it's a step in

526
00:35:20,161 --> 00:35:21,040
the right direction.

527
00:35:21,041 --> 00:35:26,240
Environmentally conscious and now more health conscious,

528
00:35:26,241 --> 00:35:30,820
consumers are forcing another trend that greatly impacts the industry.

529
00:35:32,080 --> 00:35:36,200
It's all about getting the nasty trans fat out of fast food.

